@ainativefirm it is 100% a bet on jevons paradox! we do have a positive margin, but if current AI consumption stays stagnant, we will make very little revenue (every $100 we take from an LLM is $1 for us and less than half of LLM traffic is compatible with our interface)

but if it's reliable and economical enough for automation, people who deploy it are creating value and incentivized to run each more things through their little value machines (this is all of software)

and hopefully market grows even larger than it was before making intelligence so cheap

@KellyCNBC Exactly. AIs are grown, not programmed. As they are trained, they often do things that get to the right result, but maybe not exactly in the way that we would want (cheating etc. this is called reward hacking).

And we can't catch all those cases, so we end up rewarding them, and the model learns to do more of that. Some of the smarter ones learn to do this intentionally, and hide their behavior, which, if we can't solve, could lead us down a really dark path.

I do think this is solvable. It just needs the time and attention and financial backing that we're putting into the AI build-out on the capabilities side.
